 @MrMarinus18  No, I'm referring to the "wholesalers" in the distribution chain. Retailers are really the meat in the sandwich. And it's not about holding existing stock back. If the market's a sceptical one, it's just throttling inventory throughput to create a "lack of demand" at one end and a "shortage" at the other. But, because we're talking about an "expert" market, there's plenty of gullibility to go around and all it takes is for an empty claim (e.g. a shortage for a variety of conflicting reasons) to be voiced by an "expert" in the context of something that would seem to make the claim make sense (the marginal rise of interest in crypto, CoVID-19, etc.). If anything, there's unlikely to be a significant change in sales (which would have plummeted due to limited availability in a real shortage); just hiked up prices based on a bit of creative "speculation" that seems like it might well be related to current events. 

As for the crypto story, I'd need a lot more specifics. You're talking about something which is of interest to only a tiny proportion of the community and which has a notoriety for devastating volatility and legendary unreliability in terms of value. So, supposing the crypto miner population increased tenfold and they all wanted 80 video cards each. I doubt that would generate enough sales to be more than a drop in the ocean next to the gaming market and this is reflected in the prices of specialist mining cards that do a better job of it than a graphics card but sell for ten times the price, each; to people who are expecting to buy upwards of eight cards per server blade if they want to be remotely competitive in crypto currency production. Those aren't mass production prices. They're niche market prices and highly indicative of how tiny the crypto mining impact is on gfx card availability.

For background, in terms of those engaged in dodgy trade practices, we are talking about the people who purchased a misrun of 15 inch monitors with the viewing area of a 14 inch monitor and dumped them on the Australian market (circa 1998); who dumped defective hard disks (every one had bad sectors) on the Australian market (circa 2001), who dumped defective CCD products (no capacity to render a large portion of the green spectrum) on the Australian market (circa 2003) - and I'm just getting warmed up - just because they could get away with it and it made them money on defective stock which had been written off; all stuff hijacked off the garbage truck before it got to the landfill. The question is not why would they lie about market conditions as a means of justifying a bit of price gouging but what's stopping them.

